          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            – where one understands every piece of data (every “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"}> term
            ”) as being of a given
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            which specifies its operational behaviour –
            %em identity types
            (maybe better:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em identification types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X
            are the types of those terms which serve as “witnesses” or “certificates” (see at “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"}> propositions as types
            ”) of identification of terms of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p#WhatExactlyThisMeansDepends
            What exactly this means depends on the nature of the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and the choices for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            of the identity types (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#IdeaStrictIdentityTypes"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
          %p
            But the power of the notion of identity types goes beyond this classical situation and results from the fact that they may give the notion of equality a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            meaning (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"}> propositional equality
            ”). Taking this constructive principle of identity types to its logical conclusion, leads – notably in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            %a{:href => "#IdeaMLIdentityTypes"} below
            – to identity types which themselves have identity types, reflecting identifications-of-identification, and so on, paralleling the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structure"} higher structure
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies+of+homotopies"} homotopies of homotopies
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theories"} homotopy type theories
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#note_on_terminology Note on terminology
          %p There are many different names used for this particular dependent type, as well as many different names used for the terms of this dependent type. These include the following:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th name of type
                %th name of terms
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} identity type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} identities
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} path type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} paths
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} identification type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} identifications
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equality type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equalities
          %p These four names have different reasons behind the use of the name:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The name “identity type” comes from the fact that the identity type is the canonical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-to-one+correspondence"} one-to-one correspondence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A, b:A \vdash \mathrm{Id}_A(a, b)
                of the identity
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+type+theory"} equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                        %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{id}_A:A \simeq A
                on a type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+of+indiscernibles"} identity of indiscernibles
            %li
              %p
                The name “path type” comes from either the fact that the identity type is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+object"} path space object
                in the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            %li
              %p The name “identification type” comes from the fact that elements represents identification certificates in computing, as well as philosophically that it represents the notion of “identifying” two objects together.
            %li
              %p
                The name “equality type” comes from the fact that it is the typal equivalent of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
                from
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic+with+equality"} first order logic with equality
          %h3#IdeaMLIdentityTypes ML identity types
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            for identity types in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (ML identity types) may be understood as follows (exposition and diagrams follow
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+Quantum+Gates+in+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} Myers et al. 2023
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong (0) – There is a notion of identification.
            To start with, for every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, x_2 : X
            of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %em type of identifications
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X(x_1,x_2)
            (other common notation for identity types is “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 =_{{}_X} x_2
            ” which, when adopted, requires authors to make up new symbols, like
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≡
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            this means that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
            rule for identity types as shown on the left here:
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-0-220927.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              Beware that in type theory generally and specifically in this entry further below, the self-identification certificate
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
              is often denoted “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi refl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl
              ”, alluding to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexivity
              of an equality relation.
          %p
            On the right we are indicating, here and in the following, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            on the left, under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} between dependent type theory and LCC category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundles"} bundles
            of types, their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+model+category"}> model
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            for ease of illustration, but its existence is not required by the rules for ML identity types:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^I
            may denote an object that does not arise as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+identity+types"} cubical identity types
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#CubicalIdentityTypes"} below
          %p
            The archetypical example is the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"}> classical model structure on
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimplicialSets"} SimplicialSets
            (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = \Delta[1]
            the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-simplex"} 1-simplex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            shown are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrations"} Kan fibrations
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            which may be thought of as models for (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+fibration"}> fibrations of
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-groupoids
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} injective model structure on simplicial presheaves
            over any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+site"} simplicial site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-toposes
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-stacks
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Now to consider three “self-evident” properties of the notion of identifications (Latin quotes from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            1700,
            %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/diephilosophisc00gerhgoog/page/228/mode/2up"} p. 228
            and
            %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/diephilosophisc00gerhgoog/page/228/mode/2up"} p. 230
            in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Gerhard1890"} Gerhard 1890
          %p
            %br/
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 30px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OriginalLeibnizQuote-SelfIdentification.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            %strong (I) – All data is identified with itself.
          %p
            This “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+law+of+thought"}> first law of thought
            ” is the
            = succeed "-rule" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} term introduction
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-1-220927.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %p
            %br/
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 30px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OriginalLeibnizQuote-SalvaVeritate.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            %strong (IIa) – Substitution of identifications preserves computations.
            This is Leibniz’s “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/salva+veritate"}> salva veritate
            ”-principle phrased operationally/constructively:
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-2-220928.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            this has come to be known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is that of path lifting in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrations"} Kan fibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibrations"} discrete fibrations
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"}> covering spaces
            ) underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+vector+bundles"} flat vector bundles
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"}> local systems
            ”) is the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromy"} monodromy
            of the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
          %p
            %br/
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 30px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OriginalLeibnizQuote-Composition.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p#UniquenessAxiomInIntroduction
            %strong (IIb) – An identification identifies itself with a self-identification.
            While one may argue that this is still “self-evident”, it is more subtle than the previous two principles. In particular, it concerns an
            = succeed "," do
              %em identification of identifications
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of identifications, such as
