          %p
            In type theory language, the existence of these identifications-of-identifications is the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{x'  : X}{\coprod}
            denoting the
            = succeed "-type):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-3-220928b.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %p#SemanticsOfUniquenessAxiomInSimplicialSets
            (An intuitive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is schematically indicated on the right, with the certificate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
            pictured as a copy of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            on the bottom together with a “path-of-paths” relating the concatenated path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo •
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \bullet id_x
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            or any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-simplices"} 1-simplices
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                  %munder
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^I \underset{ev_0}{\times} \{x\}
            when
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} hom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjointly
            regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-simplices"} 2-simplices
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo •
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \bullet id_x
            — although after we have identity types with all of their rules, it can be proved equivalent.)
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              (J)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇔
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Leftrightarrow
              (II) – Id-induction.
            Applying the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            rule (IIa) to the identifications-of-identifications provided by the composition rule (IIb) yields the following “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-rule"}> J-rule
            ”, which was not known to the forefathers:
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-4-220928.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %p
            (This “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"}> induction
            \-rule” for identity types was proposed in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLof75"} Martin-Löf 1975, §1.7 and p. 94
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
            is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#NordströmPeterssonSmith90"} Nordström, Petersson &amp; Smith 1990, §8.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination+rule"} term elimination rule
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "identity+type#ExplicitDefinition"} below
            = succeed "-principle" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "identity+type#InTermsOfInductiveTypes"} further below
          %p
            While the J-rule is naturally understood as the application of the transport rule (IIa) to the identifications-of-identification provided by the composition rule (IIb), it also implies both these rules, hence is equivalent to their combination (IIa and IIb). (This fact was briefly mentioned by
            %a{:href => "#Coquand11"} Coquand 2011, slides 26+28
            and amplified by
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#LadymanPresnell15"} Ladyman &amp; Presnell 2015
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Götz18"} Götz 2018, §4.2
          %p
            Importantly, as indicated on the right above, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-rule"} J-rule
            is manifestly that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi diag
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \xrightarrow{diag} X^I
            against all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
            of identity types by “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/very+good+path+space+objects"} very good path space objects
            ” in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category+theory"} model category theory
            (cf.
            %a{:href => "#Shulman12"} Shulman 2012
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "http://home.sandiego.edu/~shulman/hottminicourse2012/03models.pdf#page=34"} III, Slide 34
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Riehl22"} Riehl 2022, §1.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"}> homotopy type theory
            ” with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
                locally cartesian closed
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            %br/
          %p#PathLiftingInIdeaSection
            Incidentally, another immediate consequence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-rule"} J-rule
            is the following refinement of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport"} transport
            rule (IIa), which is closer to what Leibniz may have recognized:
          %p
            %strong (IIa’) – Substitution of identifications preserves identifications.
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MetaLogicOfIdentifications-5-220928b.jpg", :width => "760"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#CubicalIdentityTypes Cubical identity types
          %p
            Besides the Martin-Löf identity types
            %a{:href => "#IdeaMLIdentityTypes"} above
            there are other version of identity types considered in the literature, notably there are variants which share the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy-theoretic
            interpretation but differ in some technical details:
          %p
            For instance,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+identity+types"} cubical identity types
            (such as Swan identity types) are the identity types in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            they
            %em provably
            (computationally) satisfy the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
          %p
            Namely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-rule"} J-rule
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            %a{:href => "#IdeaMLIdentityTypes"} above
            still involves an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            in its statement that the lifted term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\sigma}
            is
            %em equal
            to the given term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            after restriction to self-identitfication. Cubical identity types essentially turns this equality itself into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theory"} higher observational type theory
            has its appropriate version of higher identity types.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Notice that higher identity types restricted specifically to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            behave again more like ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            does (by definition of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
          %p
            More specifically, there are also
            %em strict
            identity types, strictly without an homotopy-theoretic interpretation:
          %h3#IdeaStrictIdentityTypes Strict identity types
          %p
            Since there are two notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            in type theory, there are similarly two notions of the J-rule in type theory. The
            %strong judgmental J-rule
            states that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} refl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(x, x, \mathrm{refl}(x))
            is judgmentally equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(x)
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} refl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(x, x, \mathrm{refl}(x)) = c(x)
            %strong typal J-rule
            above, which states that there is a dependent term
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} refl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} refl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(x):Id_{C(x,x,\mathrm{refl}(x))}(J(x, x, \mathrm{refl}(x)), c(x))
          %p
            Identity types which satisfy the judgmental J-rule can be called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong strict identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %strong weak identity types
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "type+universe#TarskiStyle"} Tarski universe
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Definition Definition in Martin-Löf type theory
          %p
            The definition of identity types was originally given in explicit form by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"} Martin-Löf
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ExplicitDefinition"}> with inference rules
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfInductiveTypes"} in terms of inductive types
          %h3#ExplicitDefinition With inference rules
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            for forming identity types and terms are as follows.
          %p
            First the rule that defines the identity type itself, as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A:Type}
                  {\Gamma, x:A, y:A \vdash Id_A(x,y):Type}
          %p Now the basic “introduction” rule, which says that everything is equal to itself in a canonical way.
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} term introduction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A:Type}
                  {\Gamma, x:A \vdash r(x) : Id_A(x,x)}
          %p
            To a category theorist, it might be more natural to call this
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(x)
            indicates that this is a canonical proof of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexivity
            of equality.
          %p
            Then there are the “elimination” rule and the “computation” or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            rule of identity types, which together result in the induction principle of identity types. There are two different ways to express the elimination and computation rule of identity types, depending on whether one fixes a particular term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            for the rules or whether one leaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
            throughout the rules (Cf
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#UFP13"} UFP13
            %strong path induction
            (
            %a{:href => "#UFP13"}> UFP13
            ) or the
            %strong standard J rule
            (
            %a{:href => "#KrausRaumer19"}> Kraus and Raumer (2019)
            ), while the former results in
            %strong based path induction
            (
            %a{:href => "#UFP13"}> UFP13
            ) or the
            %strong Paulin-Mohring J rule
            (
            %a{:href => "#KrausRaumer19"}> Kraus and Raumer (2019)
            ), since the principle is based at a particular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            and was first defined in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#PaulinMohring93"} Paulin-Mohring (1993)
          %p
            The following rules may seem a little ad-hoc, but they are actually a particular case of the general notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
          %h4#standard_j_rule Standard J rule
          %p Then we have the “elimination” rule, which is easily the most subtle and powerful.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %mrow
                            %mo ̲
                          %mo .
                          %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma, x:A, y:A, p:Id_A(x,y), \Delta(x,y,p) \vdash C(x,y,p):Type \quad \Gamma, x:A, \Delta(x,x,r(x)) \vdash t(x):C(x,x,r(x))}
                  {\Gamma, x:A, y:A, p:Id_A(x,y), \Delta(x,y,p) \vdash J^{\underline{ }.t}(x,y,p) : C(x,y,p)}
          %p
            Ignore the presence of the additional context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            for now; it is unnecessary if we also have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
          %ol
